- How would you leverage hardware intrinsics for popcount on supported platforms?
- How would you scale the approach to 256-bit or 512-bit widths?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of fast, branchless bit tricks and constant-time bit-scanning utilities common in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- Isolating least-significant 1: v = n & -n
- De Bruijn-based indexing: map a 5-bit index to a trailing-zero count
- Handling edge case n == 0 (return 32)
- Static lookup table of 32 entries

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t countTrailingZeros32(uint32_t n){
  static const int debruijn[32] = {0,1,28,2,29,14,24,3,30,22,20,15,25,17,4,8,31,27,13,23,21,19,16,7,26,12,18,9,6,11,5,10};
  if(n == 0) return 32;
  uint32_t v = n & -n;
  return debruijn[(v * 0x077CB531) >> 27];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- What are alternatives that avoid a static table, e.g., using binary search on bit groups?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate multi-word integers with precise bit logic, no loops, and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit two-word shifts without loops
- x & ~(x<<1) to locate run-start bits
- 64-bit popcount for final tally

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t countOneRuns128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t lo_shift = lo << 1;
  uint64_t hi_shift = (hi << 1) | (lo >> 63);
  uint64_t mask_lo = lo & ~lo_shift;
  uint64_t mask_hi = hi & ~hi_shift;
  return __builtin_popcountll(mask_lo) + __builtin_popcountll(mask_hi);
}
```

